{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>But if he annul, etc.</b> The reason the verse repeated to say <b>he shall annul — he annuls</b>, and also the inclusiveness of <b>all that proceeds</b>, will be explained based on what they said in Nedarim chapter 11 (Nedarim 82b), and this is their language: Rav Yehudah said, etc. She vowed [regarding] two loaves — with one she fasts and with one she does not fast; since he annuls the one she fasts, he also annuls the one she does not fast — end quote. And this is what the verse intends in the doubling <b>but if he annul — he annuls</b>, meaning: if he annuls the portion that is subject to annulment, ״he annuls״ — to include also the portion that is not subject to the law of annulment. And it says <b>all that proceeds from her lips</b>, etc., meaning: the reason he also annuls the portion that is not subject to the law of annulment is because it is bundled together with something that is subject to annulment, and when he annuls, he annuls them all together. And according to the opinion of Rav Asi who disagreed there in Nedarim, and this is his language: Rav Asi said, he annuls the one she fasts but does not annul the one she does not fast — end quote, it will be explained on this basis: ״if he annul״ — meaning, something that is subject to the law of annulment; ״he annuls״ — meaning, his annulment takes effect, but not the portion that is not subject to the law of annulment. And its saying <b>all</b>, etc., <b>of her vows</b>, meaning: even when they are bundled together, two things — one with which she fasts and one with which she does not fast." "<b>Further,</b> it will be explained based on what they said there in the Mishnah (Nedarim 87a), and this is their language: ״I know that there are those who may annul, but I did not know that this is a vow״ — Rabbi Meir says: he may not annul; and the Sages say: he may annul — end quote. And this is what the verse intends in the statement <b>he shall annul — he annuls</b>, meaning: even if his error was only concerning annulment, it avails him to annul later when he learns that there are those who may annul — this is according to the Sages. And according to Rabbi Meir, the doubling will be explained to say that there is a situation where even though he did not annul in time, he may annul not in time — namely, where he did not know that there are those who may annul. And its saying <b>of her vows</b>, according to Rabbi Meir's opinion, is resolved on this basis: when did I tell you that he may annul afterwards? — when his error was not about vows, and that is its saying <b>of her vows</b>, meaning: when he recognizes her vows. And according to the Sages, its saying ״of her vows״ will be explained as: recognition of the fact that they are vows — you have thus learned that his error was regarding [the vow itself], and even so the verse expanded annulment for him, to say that he may annul once he knows that they are vows."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:16:01.194798691Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}